1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,545
Can a phone flag a defect and tell an operator
what to do? Here’s SurfaceNet, working offline.

2
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:11,196
In Inspect, open Rear camera. A mounted phone
can enroll a clean part as its reference.

3
00:00:11,336 --> 00:00:15,582
The app checks image quality and asks
for a new capture if detail is too low.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,981
Open the annotated defect. The app highlights the

5
00:00:18,981 --> 00:00:22,109
mark, estimates its severity,
and sends it to review.

6
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:27,062
For a live hold, the rear light can test
whether the same defect pixels persist.

7
00:00:27,202 --> 00:00:32,930
Here, the phone replay uses simulated lighting.
Drag the divider to compare the registered captures.

8
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:39,208
Tap Ask local Llama. Llama 3.2 runs
on the Arm CPU through ExecuTorch.

9
00:00:39,348 --> 00:00:43,687
For this mark, it recommends keeping the
part segregated and checking pit depth.

10
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,975
Enter the part ID and save. Trace log reopens
the mask, finding and Llama recommendation.

11
00:00:50,115 --> 00:00:53,385
Records stay on the device
and can be exported as CSV.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,030
Under Device, compare both vision profiles
and export separate live-camera timings.

13
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:07,716
Across 1,004 held-out images, four-view
INT8 reached 97.45% image AUROC.

14
00:01:07,856 --> 00:01:14,997
Both profiles flagged 105 of 110 defects.
Four views flagged 35 fewer clean images.

15
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:20,400
Next we validate sustained throughput and
rear-light repeatability on the physical handset.
